<%# Where else you can go. Four destinations, always all four, in the same order
    on every screen — a reader who found `Days` on the front door finds it in
    the same place in the gallery.

    Locals:
      here: which of the four this screen IS, or nil when it is none of them.
            `ApplicationHelper#compass_destinations` owns the contract and
            raises on anything else.
      rail: default false. True on /feed, where this is not inside the masthead
            but is a sticky bar of its own. The class is `.compass`, not
            `.masthead__compass`, precisely because of that — an element that
            lives in two places should not be named after one of them.

    The current destination stays in the row rather than being dropped from it.
    Rendering only "the other three" was the first draft, and it moves every
    label one slot to the left depending on which screen you are standing on,
    which is the opposite of learning the set once.

    It keeps `.caps-link`. Same size, same tracking, same 44px box as its
    neighbours; only the colour changes and only the `<a>` is missing. As a bare
    `<span>` it drops out of small caps into body type and reads as a bug.

    Nothing here is per-visitor and nothing here is counted, which is what lets
    this render inside the publicly cached HTML of `/` and `/days` (story 0007).
%>
<nav class="compass <%= "compass--rail" if local_assigns[:rail] %>" aria-label="Sections">
  <% compass_destinations(here).each do |destination| %>
    <% if destination[:current] %>
      <span class="caps-link compass__here" aria-current="page"><%= destination[:label] %></span>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to destination[:label], destination[:path], class: "caps-link" %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</nav>
